\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Over 7,000 Nurses Go on Strike At Two New York City Hospitals\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ginger Adams Otis \par \uc2 305 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 January 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 More than 7,000 nurses at two New York City hospitals went on strike Monday after contract talks broke down over pay and staffing levels.\par \par \uc2 Roughly 3,500 nurses at \uc2 Montefiore Medical Center\uc2  in the Bronx and approximately 3,600 nurses at \uc2 Mount Sinai Hospital\uc2  in Manhattan walked off the job as of 6 a.m., according to the \uc2 New York State Nurses Association\uc2 , which represents the striking workers.\par \par \uc2 The labor action comes after late-night bargaining between \uc2 NYSNA\uc2  representatives and both hospitals failed to produce tentative agreements, the union said. Both hospitals said \uc2 NYSNA\uc2  had turned down a 19.1% compounded wage increase over the duration of the contract and offers to improve staffing that its nurses at other facilities had accepted.\par \par \uc2 "We will not give up on our fight to ensure that our patients have enough nurses at the bedside," the union said Monday.\par \par \uc2 Montefiore said its hospital remains open but its elective surgeries and procedures have been canceled, along with appointments at its ambulatory centers. The hospital said it promised to create over 170 new nursing positions, yet "\uc2 NYSNA\uc2  leadership has decided to walk away from their patients."\par \par \uc2 Mount Sinai\uc2  said its procedures continued as usual on Monday. The hospital said it made some changes ahead of the strike, such as diverting ambulances to other locations, transferring newborn babies who need intensive care and arranging for cancer patients to find alternative ways to get treatment.\par \par \uc2 The two hospitals have more than 1,000 beds between them, according to \uc2 NYSNA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  on Sunday urged the union and hospitals to enter into arbitration to resolve the contract dispute. Arbitration would allow a third-party arbitrator to decide the terms of the contract.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230110ej1a0001f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
