\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Robust Job Market, Pay Growth Show Signs of Cooling\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bryan Mena \par \uc2 755 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 January 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The labor market proved to be a resilient stabilizer in 2022 for a U.S. economy facing the highest inflation in four decades.\par \par \uc2 With the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  having raised interest rates at the fastest pace since the early 1980s, however, the economy has slowed, and effects of that are filtering into hiring and wages.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate was 3.7% in November, according to the Labor Department, just above half-century lows matched earlier in 2022. Fed officials forecast the rate to rise to 4.6% in the fourth quarter of 2023, in economic projections released last month.\par \par \uc2 Here is what underpinned the labor market's resilience in 2022, and what lies ahead in 2023.\par \par \uc2 Hiring pace\par \par \uc2 U.S. employers added an average of 392,000 jobs a month in 2022 through November, according to Labor Department figures. While slower than in 2021, the hiring pace was more than double that of 2019, the year before the pandemic began.\par \par \uc2 Gains were led by leisure and hospitality, which includes restaurants, hotels and tourist attractions. The industry accounted for about 1 in 5 net jobs added in the first 11 months of 2022. Job gains were also strong in other service sectors.\par \par \uc2 "Workers at the lower end of the income distribution in areas like leisure and hospitality, transportation, and retail did particularly well," said \uc2 Aneta Markowska\uc2 , chief financial economist at Jefferies. "Demand for services was just enormous."\par \par \uc2 The pace of hiring cooled in the second half of 2022, and several large employers laid off workers or planned to cut jobs. While those moves didn't result in an increase in broad layoff figures, economists surveyed in the fall by The Wall Street Journal forecast employers to start shedding jobs in 2023.\par \par \uc2 Wage growth\par \par \uc2 Wages grew at a historically strong rate in 2022, though the gains failed to keep pace with inflation. Average hourly earnings for private-sector employers rose 5.1% in November from a year earlier, while consumer prices climbed 7.1% during the same period, according to the Labor Department.\par \par \uc2 Median wages for leisure and hospitality workers advanced 7% in the 12 months ended in November, the most of any industry tracked by the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Atlanta\uc2 . Wages in the trade and transportation and the manufacturing sectors grew more than overall pay.\par \par \uc2 "The fastest wage growth was where there was this combination of strong demand for workers, relatively reticent labor supply and lower pay," said \uc2 Nick Bunker\uc2 , an economist at the jobs site Indeed.\par \par \uc2 Lower-paying sectors have seen a slowdown in wage growth, Mr. Bunker said, referring to Indeed's wage tracker.\par \par \uc2 That tracker showed wage growth in 82% of industries was lower in November than six months earlier. If pay changes remain on the same trajectory, wage growth will return to prepandemic levels by the second half of 2023, Mr. Bunker said.\par \par \uc2 Labor supply\par \par \uc2 One factor supporting wage growth in 2022 was the struggle many employers had in finding workers.\par \par \uc2 Job openings exceeded the number of people who were unemployed and seeking work throughout 2022, according to the Labor Department, though the gap began to narrow. The labor-force participation rate, or the share of workers who are employed or seeking a job, hasn't recovered to its prepandemic level.\par \par \uc2 "The labor-force participation rate is likely to continue to trend down over time," said \uc2 Stephen Stanley\uc2 , chief economist at \uc2 Amherst Pierpont Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Some of the decline in labor supply can be attributed to early retirements triggered by the pandemic. Mr. Stanley said that those who retired early aren't likely to return to the labor force.\par \par \uc2 The participation rate for prime-age workers -- those between the ages of 25 and 54 -- approached prepandemic levels in the summer but has edged down in recent months.\par \par \uc2 Jobless rate\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate in 2022 fell to match the half-century lows set in 2019 and early 2020. The November rate was up slightly from the 3.5% low for 2022.\par \par \uc2 Some private economists expect the unemployment rate to rise more than the Fed projects.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Markowska of Jefferies said she expects the unemployment rate to reach about 5% by the end of 2023 and then continue rising in the following year. Economists from \uc2 Nomura\uc2  forecast a 5.9% unemployment rate in late 2023.\par \par \uc2 "Since there isn't much upside for labor-force growth, any upward pressure on unemployment will more likely come from job losses," said Ms. Markowska.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw380\pich477\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal5700\pichgoal7155
