\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: California Judge Puts Hold on Fast-Food Wage Law\b0\par\par\uc2 By Heather Haddon \par \uc2 395 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 December 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A state judge put on hold a California law that could raise minimum wages for fast-food workers until a hearing can determine whether it should take effect in January.\par \par \uc2 Save Local Restaurants, a group of franchisee and restaurant business associations, filed suit Thursday against the state's Department of Industrial Relations challenging its intention to implement the law Jan. 1.\par \par \uc2 The group submitted a petition in early December for a 2024 ballot measure that would overturn the law. The secretary of state's office is currently determining whether the petition has about 623,000 valid signatures, in which case the law's implementation would be postponed until the ballot measure vote.\par \par \uc2 In its lawsuit, Save Local Restaurants asked the court to block the state from beginning to implement the law until the secretary of state's office finishes the petition validation process.\par \par \uc2 Sacramento County Superior Court Judge Shelleyanne W.L. Chang on Friday granted a temporary restraining order for the implementation of the new law, writing that the court couldn't consider the matter in such a short time frame. The court is set to hold a hearing on the injunction on Jan. 13.\par \par \uc2 "While this pause is temporary, the impact is beyond just one piece of legislation and keeps intact for the time being California's century-old referendum process," the Save Local Restaurants coalition said.\par \par \uc2 Erin Mellon, a spokeswoman for Gov. \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , said the secretary of state hasn't certified that the industry coalition has enough signatures to qualify for the ballot and it has an obligation to implement the law unless that occurs. The state, however, will abide by Friday's court order, she said.\par \par \uc2 The California law is the first of its kind in the U.S. and has emerged as a high-stake battle between organized labor and fast-food chains and operators.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Newsom, a Democrat, signed the law in September. It creates a 10-person government-appointed council made up of workers, employers, union representatives and business advocates that could set the minimum wage for the fast-food industry as high as $22 an hour in 2023 and establish new workplace standards.\par \par \uc2 Currently California's minimum wage is $15 an hour, and is set to rise to $15.50 in 2023.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020221231eicv0002f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
