\par\par \b \uc2 Axed Tech Workers Find Jobs Quickly\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sarah Chaney Cambon and Gwynn Guilford \par \uc2 1,282 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 December 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Most laid off tech workers are finding jobs shortly after beginning their search, a new survey shows, as employers continue to scoop up workers in a tight labor market.\par \par \uc2 About 79% of workers recently hired after a tech-company layoff or termination landed their new job within three months of starting their search, according to a \uc2 ZipRecruiter\uc2  survey of new hires. That was just below the 83% share of all laid-off workers who were re-employed in the same time frame.\par \par \uc2 Nearly four in 10 previously laid off tech workers found jobs less than a month after they began searching, \uc2 ZipRecruiter\uc2  found in the survey.\par \par \uc2 "Despite the widespread layoffs, hiring freezes, and cost-cutting taking place in tech, many tech workers are finding re-employment remarkably quickly," said \uc2 Julia Pollak\uc2 , chief economist at \uc2 ZipRecruiter\uc2 . "They're still the most sought-after workers with the most in-demand skills."\par \par \uc2 Job openings across the economy -- at 10.3 million -- are down from record highs but far exceed the number of unemployed Americans, providing opportunities for workers who lose their jobs and those who choose to seek another.\par \par \uc2 Workers previously employed in other industries, including entertainment and leisure, transportation and delivery, and manufacturing, also found new jobs quickly, the \uc2 ZipRecruiter\uc2  data showed.\par \par \uc2 The job market for tech workers is slowing as the broader economy falters under the pressure of Federal Reserve interest-rate increases and high inflation. Layoffs and hiring freezes are occurring at startups and large tech companies such as \uc2 Amazon.com Inc\uc2 . and \uc2 Facebook\uc2  parent \uc2 Meta Platforms Inc\uc2 . that hired aggressively early on in the pandemic. The cuts are hitting workers in tech jobs -- such as software engineers -- and other corporate roles including recruiters.\par \par \uc2 Still, tech firms making cuts are outnumbered by those that are hiring.\par \par \uc2 A smaller share of tech workers is spending long periods searching for work after a layoff. About 5% of laid-off tech workers who found jobs from April to October had spent more than six months hunting for work, down from 26% of those hired between August 2021 and February 2022, \uc2 ZipRecruiter\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Wen Huber, 23 years old, was laid off from a videographer job at a real-estate tech startup in late July. Mr. Huber, who lives near Seattle, thought it would take a while to land a new position, given he didn't have a four-year-college degree and many other job seekers were flooding the tech job market.\par \par \uc2 "When I was applying, to be honest, I didn't feel very confident because there was such an influx of competition with a lot of people also being laid off," he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Huber had built up a savings buffer, allowing him to be more selective in his job hunt as he sought to pivot into social media. He documented his unemployment experience in a series of videos on \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2 . The videos helped him land an interview -- and ultimately an offer -- for a social-media manager job at a software startup, Mr. Huber said.He started in September.\par \par \uc2 "I was surprised at how quickly I was able to secure an offer for a job," he said.\par \par \uc2 Short job searches in tech have become slightly less common as the labor market slows from earlier in the year. Among people who recently lost a job and worked in tech previously, 37% found a new position within one month of starting to look, according to \uc2 ZipRecruiter\uc2 . That compared with 50% in February's survey.\par \par \uc2 "We've definitely seen a slowdown in hiring, but the reason why is that the job creation level was beyond record highs because of the slingshot effect of the pandemic," said Ryan Sutton, district president at Robert Half, a global recruitment firm. "From August 2020 to May 2022, it wasn't red-hot. It was lava-hot."\par \par \uc2 Usually when mass layoffs hit, there is an influx of tech candidates contacting his company to help with their job search, Mr. Sutton said.\par \par \uc2 "We have not seen it yet -- we haven't seen more candidates coming to market," he said. "Our recruiters are having to hunt and hustle just as much as they had to in the last couple of years."\par \par \uc2 Client firms in tech also haven't mentioned any plans to cut jobs, Mr. Sutton said.\par \par \uc2 About 74% of workers recently hired after losing or leaving a job at a tech company remained in the industry, according to \uc2 ZipRecruiter\uc2 . Others who previously worked at tech companies switched to firms in industries such as retail, financial services and healthcare in the six months ending in mid-October.\par \par \uc2 Pinnacol Assurance\uc2 , a 650-person workers' compensation insurer, saw a 46% increase in job candidates from big tech companies including \uc2 Meta\uc2 , \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  and \uc2 Twitter\uc2  between September and mid-December, said Tim Johnson, the company's chief human resources officer.\par \par \uc2 The influx of applicants has helped Pinnacol fill tech-related roles such as data scientist, machine-learning engineer and cloud architect in recent months. In mid-December, Pinnacol's recruiting team made an offer to a job candidate from \uc2 Google\uc2 , Mr. Johnson said.\par \par \uc2 Ayanna Chapman, 42, started a systems-engineering job at Pinnacol overseeing its computer systems in mid-November after she was laid off from another systems-engineer position this spring.\par \par \uc2 A generous severance package allowed her to take several months to freshen skills and study for certifications including in the Python programming language. When the Atlanta-area resident began looking for work in the second week of October, recruiters quickly reached out with interview opportunities, she said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Chapman was keen on finding a job with stability and thought Pinnacol would fit. She received an offer from the Denver-based company about two weeks after beginning the interview process, much faster than previous experiences, she said.\par \par \uc2 "I literally cried tears of joy like, 'Oh, my God, I got the job. I can't believe it,'" Ms. Chapman said.\par \par \uc2 Employers broadly are responding to job candidates fast, likely for fear of losing them in a competitive market with a historically low jobless rate of 3.7%. Nine in 10 \uc2 ZipRecruiter\uc2  survey respondents said they heard back from a recruiter or hiring manager within a week of applying for a job.\par \par \uc2 ZipRecruiter\uc2 's most recent survey was drawn from 2,550 U.S. residents who had started a new job within the six months ending in mid-October. The data align with other job-market figures that signal the hot labor market is cooling.\par \par \uc2 Of the \uc2 ZipRecruiter\uc2  survey respondents who said they previously worked in tech, most of them likely worked for tech companies regardless of their occupation, Ms. Pollak said. In other words, a recruiter at \uc2 Amazon\uc2  would likely be classified as a tech-industry worker in the survey. But a data scientist at \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  would be a retail-industry worker.\par \par \uc2 Separate labor-market figures suggest employers across industries are still seeking to hire tech workers, though less so than earlier in the pandemic. Postings on job-site Indeed for tech occupations are still well above prepandemic levels, but have fallen steeply over the past year.\par \par \uc2 Software-developer job ads on Indeed are down 34% from a year earlier, and ads for mathematics roles -- which include data scientists -- are 28% lower. Overall postings are down 7.7% from a year ago.\par \par \uc2 "With tech workers, it's a much bigger pullback," said \uc2 Nick Bunker\uc2 , an economist at Indeed Hiring Lab. "It's still above prepandemic levels, but if the current trend keeps up, I don't imagine that talking point will be true anymore at some point next year."\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Doug Cameron contributed to this article.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw380\pich355\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal5700\pichgoal5325
