\par\par \b \uc2 Tesla\uc2  Suspends Work at Shanghai Factory\b0\par\par\uc2 By Raffaele Huang \par \uc2 672 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 December 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE -- \uc2 Tesla Inc\uc2 . suspended car production at its Shanghai plant on Saturday, extending a planned eight-day production halt at its largest worldwide plant by car output, according to people familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 The halt follows a recent slowdown in global demand for \uc2 Tesla\uc2  vehicles. The decision to prolong the work suspension comes as the electric-vehicle manufacturer faces a wave of Covid-19 infections among its workers and suppliers in China, the people said.\par \par \uc2 On Saturday, \uc2 Tesla\uc2  told some of its Shanghai employees that it would stop car production that day, a day earlier than originally planned, the people said. The company earlier told staff that production would resume on Jan. 2, they said.\par \par \uc2 One of the people said that while it isn't unusual for auto makers to pause work on some production lines during the Lunar New Year holiday and the summer, \uc2 Tesla\uc2  hasn't traditionally halted all car production during the Christmas holidays.\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2  has built up sufficient inventory and the production halt won't hurt its ability to fulfill orders, the people said. During the summer, \uc2 Tesla\uc2  boosted the capacity of its Shanghai plant to more than 750,000 vehicles a year. However, demand for its cars has been weaker than expected over the past two months, they said, as China's auto market turned sluggish.\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2  buyers in China currently can receive their new vehicles within four weeks, compared with September, when the wait was up to five times as long, according to the company's website.\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2  didn't respond to a request for comment. On Sunday, the company told the Global Times, a news outlet run by China's Communist Party, that it had planned for annual maintenance of its car-manufacturing lines in Shanghai this week, and that workers would take a break during the period.\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2  said it wouldn't shut the workshop that manufactures charging piles during this period, according to the Global Times report. Reuters earlier reported on the suspension.\par \par \uc2 Covid-19 infections have washed over China in recent weeks after Beijing eased its zero-Covid policies, taking down workers at car makers and their suppliers, as well as dealership staff.\par \par \uc2 Fewer potential customers have been visiting stores as worries of being infected remain elevated.\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's Shanghai plant was forced to shut down for weeks earlier this year because of Covid-19-related disruptions, prompting Wall Street to temper its expectations for the car maker's growth this year.\par \par \uc2 Demand in the world's biggest auto market has been weak since October, as Beijing's stringent Covid-19 control measures forced businesses to shut and confined potential buyers at home. Car sales in November fell 9.2% from a year earlier, according to official data.\par \par \uc2 The China Passenger Car Association on Friday forecast nationwide car sales in December to grow by 4.5% from a year earlier, a relatively modest rate given the year-end expiration of subsidies meant to make EVs more attractive. EV sales, a growth engine for China's car market, have significantly slowed since June.\par \par \uc2 In the third quarter of the year, \uc2 Tesla\uc2  said it produced 22,000 more cars than it delivered globally, fueling concerns of slack demand, especially in China. The company has lost roughly 70% of its market value since its stock hit a record in November 2021, partly as a result of anxieties around weakening demand for EVs.\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  said Thursday on a Twitter Spaces chat that he expected the global economy to fall into a "serious recession" in 2023, pulling down demand for big-ticket items. He said \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's gross profit margin has been higher than its peers, which gives it room to maintain sales growth by slashing prices. The car maker has doubled the discounts offered on its two most popular models delivered in the U.S. this month.\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's Shanghai plant accounts for more than half of the company's global car deliveries.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020221227eicr0001o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
