\par\par \b \uc2 Want to Save the Planet? Where's Your Union Card?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ken Girardin \par \uc2 790 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 December 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Albany, N.Y. -- New York state officials this week took their most serious step yet to limit the state's greenhouse-gas emissions. But they also showed they are more serious about taking care of one of the state's most entrenched special interests than about climate change.\par \par \uc2 Charging ahead with "the most aggressive clean energy and climate plan in the country," New York's 22-member Climate Action Council -- which the state Legislature assigned the critical task of deciding how to achieve emissions reductions ordered in 2019 -- on Monday adopted a final "Scoping Plan" that will serve as the basis for an avalanche of new regulations. New York law requires the state to cut its "climate pollution" significantly by 2030, and this plan is the blueprint agencies will use to crack down on specific parts of the economy.\par \par \uc2 The plan calls for prohibiting new natural-gas stoves, furnaces, and water heaters; banning new gasoline-fueled vehicles; and charging a "compliance obligation," or tax, on nearly anything that generates greenhouse gases. As part of the state's "just transition" to a decarbonized economy, the council expects New York to gain more than 300,000 "clean energy jobs" by 2040, after 58,000 "legacy" job losses of course.\par \par \uc2 But the council is also focused on a different type of green: ensuring that when homeowners and businesses get squeezed, much of that money drips into union coffers. By the council's suggestion, the jobs created as a result of the state's climate-change-mitigation efforts "should be good, family-sustaining, union jobs," and the state "should apply robust labor standards across all sectors and projects" affected by their orders.\par \par \uc2 Those "robust labor standards" would cover essentially every change the council wants imposed on New York's private economy. For one, construction projects would have "project labor agreements," which generally block nonunion contractors from bidding on jobs. As a result, ostensibly urgent efforts to make buildings more efficient or modernize the electric grid will happen more slowly and at higher cost.\par \par \uc2 Construction projects will also pay New York's "prevailing wage," under which the state requires employers to match union wages and benefit levels, as well as certain union work rules. Sea-level rise isn't worth stopping, it seems, unless laborers working on green-energy projects get at least the $94 an hour New York City's prevailing wage requires.\par \par \uc2 Companies performing any maintenance or operations related to the plan's recommendations will have to sign "labor peace agreements" with unions, deals that require the business to have a unionized workforce. New York is one of 23 states where employees lack right-to-work protections, meaning employees can be fired if they don't agree to pay union dues or fees.\par \par \uc2 The council is looking to give labor a leg up even outside directly affected sectors. An undefined swath of businesses -- potentially anyone getting tax credits or other financial relief -- will have to sign "employer neutrality agreements" under which companies can't push back if union organizers arrive on their property to make sensational promises.\par \par \uc2 Unions and their allies in government will rationalize these policies, often without evidence, by arguing that union workers are better trained or have better safety records. But the only thing missing appears to be an outright requirement that anyone working in "clean energy" carry a union membership card.\par \par \uc2 New York's unions have turned increasingly to Albany to boost membership. Only about 14% of the state's private-sector workers are unionized, down from about 25% in the mid-1980s, according to UnionStats.\par \par \uc2 Even in the traditionally union-heavy construction sector, fewer than one-third of tradesmen today are represented by unions, down from nearly half in 1983. That means prevailing-wage and project-labor-agreement mandates will disqualify about two-thirds of the state's construction workforce from this supposedly all-hands-on-deck effort to save the planet.\par \par \uc2 Hypocrisy has been a constant in New York's green-energy adventures, with the state forcing the profitable Indian Point nuclear plant to close while paying about $500 million annually to keep three money-losing plants open to preserve their "carbon-free" generation. Abundant hydroelectric power from Canada doesn't count as a "renewable" energy source in upstate New York, but it does once it reaches New York City. But nothing comes close to the Climate Action Council's double-think. Either climate change is an urgent danger, or it's so unthreatening that the state can slow and complicate its response with lucrative labor handouts. Both can't be true.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Mr. Girardin is a fellow at the Empire Center for Public Policy.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020221223eicn0000w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
