\par\par \b \uc2 Goldman Plans Big Job Cuts, Reductions in Some Bonuses\b0\par\par\uc2 By AnnaMaria Andriotis and Justin Baer \par \uc2 672 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 December 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Goldman Sachs Group Inc\uc2 . is planning to lay off several thousand employees, according to people familiar with the matter, another consequence of this year's deal-making slump.\par \par \uc2 A person familiar with the situation said the bank will be leaner in 2023, but it will still have more employees than it did before the pandemic. Goldman had some 49,000 employees as of September, up from about 38,000 at the end of 2019.\par \par \uc2 Goldman also expects to slash, and in some cases eliminate, the annual bonuses of underperforming employees, people familiar with the matter said.\par \par \uc2 Like other Wall Street banks, Goldman hired aggressively throughout 2020 and 2021, bringing in new employees to help it keep up with an M&A boom. This year was a different story: An economic slowdown, war in Europe and rising interest rates triggered a bear market for stocks and a slump in deal making. \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  also laid off workers this month, and similar cutbacks have swept through American companies.\par \par \uc2 Some of the job cuts at Goldman will be part of annual workforce reviews. In most years, Goldman eliminates underperformers during that process, but layoffs were suspended during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Taking away bonuses is another lever that Goldman can pull to trim expenses. Like most Wall Street employees, Goldman staff received a large portion of their compensation in annual payouts tied to their performance and that of the overall firm.\par \par \uc2 Employees who get no bonus usually interpret it as an invitation to leave. Eliminating bonuses for highly paid partners and other executives can also give the bank leeway to preserve compensation for junior bankers.\par \par \uc2 Semafor reported earlier on cutbacks at Goldman.\par \par \uc2 Investment bankers covering tech, including some managing directors, could be hit hard by layoffs, according to people familiar with the matter. The tech industry in particular fueled the pandemic stock run-up, and shares of tech companies are down drastically this year.\par \par \uc2 But if overhiring and overpaying staff during good times has been a standing tradition on Wall Street, then so have steep job and bonus cuts during lean years.\par \par \uc2 In 2020, the panic that gripped the markets early in the year as the coronavirus pandemic circled the globe quickly gave way a massive rally. The stock market routinely touched new highs that year and corporate deal making brought in blockbuster fees.\par \par \uc2 And the Wall Street firms, in turn, went on a hiring spree, often offering big bucks to lure new employees and keep current staff from jumping ship.\par \par \uc2 The party came to a halt this year. With uncertain markets and a recession potentially on the horizon, corporate clients went jittery. Wall Street firms have been forced to confront a steep slowdown in many of their businesses.\par \par \uc2 What's more, Goldman's prominence in investment banking and trading has made its results -- and its stock price -- more sensitive to the ebb and flow of the capital markets than many of its peers.\par \par \uc2 Banks such as \uc2 JPMorgan Chase & Co\uc2 . have consumer-lending operations that dwarf Goldman's. And in the years since the financial crisis of 2008-09, \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 's push into wealth and asset management has left it less dependent on deal fees than rival Goldman.\par \par \uc2 Goldman in October announced a reshuffling of its businesses, part of a push to draw more attention to its own asset and wealth management businesses, where it can clip relatively steady fees.\par \par \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  earlier this month cut about 2% of its global workforce, representing some 1,600 jobs. The layoffs followed the company's acquisitions of discount broker E*Trade and fund manager \uc2 Eaton Vance\uc2  that resulted in an additional roughly 20,000 employees joining.\par \par \uc2 "We went from boom to bust immediately, so in hindsight [banks] probably overhired," said Alan Johnson, managing director at Johnson Associates, a compensation consulting firm specializing in the financial services industry.\par \par \uc2 Next year "isn't looking great either," Mr. Johnson said.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Peter Rudegeair contributed to this article.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich448\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal6720
