\par\par \b \uc2 Business & Technology: Companies Speed Up Their Hiring Process --- Some drop interviews for hourly and temporary roles amid worker shortage\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sarah Chaney Cambon \par \uc2 1,032 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 December 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Some employers racing to snap up workers in the tight labor market are omitting a step once considered crucial to hiring: the job interview.\par \par \uc2 United Parcel Service Inc\uc2 . has bulked up its staff without conducting job interviews for package handlers and seasonal drivers. \uc2 Home Depot Inc\uc2 . has sent job offers as quickly as 24 hours after candidates apply, in some cases eliminating the interview process. \uc2 Gap Inc\uc2 . has dropped job interviews for some warehousing positions.\par \par \uc2 Many of the positions that don't require interviews are hourly and in sectors with elevated rates of turnover, such as warehousing and retail. The abandonment of the job interview, while still a rarity, is happening at companies trying to fill certain roles as the labor market slowly cools from its rebound from the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Workers remain in high demand, particularly in blue-collar fields -- while recent layoff announcements from companies such as \uc2 Amazon.com Inc\uc2 ., \uc2 Walmart Inc\uc2 . and \uc2 Ford Motor Co\uc2 . are hitting white-collar workers.\par \par \uc2 Employers added 263,000 jobs in November and the unemployment rate held at 3.7%, adding to signs the overall job market continues to run strong. Job openings -- at 10.3 million in October -- far exceeded the 6.1 million unemployed Americans seeking work that month. A tight labor market during the pandemic rebound also has prompted employers to drop college-degree requirements as well as background checks and experience requirements.\par \par \uc2 Many businesses feel they have exhausted methods for attracting workers, including pay raises, signing bonuses and larger benefit packages, said \uc2 Julia Pollak\uc2 , chief economist at jobs site \uc2 ZipRecruiter\uc2 . Now, their focus is on moving fast.\par \par \uc2 "Speed is the number one way companies are competing for hourly workers at the moment, especially for the holiday season," Ms. Pollak said.\par \par \uc2 She said the phenomenon is cropping up in healthcare, as well. Some organizations are using nursing licenses in lieu of job interviews to make on-the-spot offers. A job offer without an interview might previously be a clear indication of a scam. But large, legitimate employers are adopting the approach increasingly, she said.\par \par \uc2 Keontae Starks, 22 years old, experienced the fast hiring process firsthand. He began searching for work this fall but found that many of the jobs near where he lives in Baldwyn, Miss., required experience he didn't have.\par \par \uc2 "How am I supposed to grow as a worker if all these jobs require that I have one or two years of manufacturing experience or automotive experience?" Mr. Starks said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Starks came across a job ad on \uc2 Indeed.com\uc2  for a \uc2 UPS\uc2  package-handling role, which he said didn't have extreme qualifications such as three to four years of factory experience. He decided to fill out an application.\par \par \uc2 He quickly received a job offer, without an interview, for a package-handler position that involved sorting packages onto trucks for delivery during the holiday season.\par \par \uc2 "It was just fill out a form and then boom, you knew if you got it or not," he said. "I was very surprised. It was just very, very fast."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Starks started the job in Tupelo last month. He worked an inconsistent number of days and early-morning shifts with varying start and end times, including an overnight shift starting at midnight. He said he recently left \uc2 UPS\uc2  to look for a full-time, permanent job with a more routine schedule.\par \par \uc2 About 80% of \uc2 UPS\uc2  seasonal workers, including warehouse workers and some delivery drivers, are hired without an interview and can get a job offer within 25 minutes of applying, said Matt Lavery, \uc2 UPS\uc2  director of global sourcing, recruiting and onboarding.\par \par \uc2 The company also waives interviews for some of its permanent positions, including package-handler jobs.\par \par \uc2 The shipping powerhouse set out to recruit more than 100,000 seasonal workers this year and found it a little harder to do so than it did last year, said Mr. Lavery.\par \par \uc2 Job candidates still have many choices in this labor market, he said.\par \par \uc2 "They are making their voice heard either by accepting job offers or not even applying or just ghosting you in some cases," Mr. Lavery said.\par \par \uc2 Hourly workers are essential to the production process, said Sean Behr, chief executive at Fountain, a company whose automation software speeds up the job-application process for hourly workers.\par \par \uc2 If warehouses are understaffed, companies could require existing employees to work overtime while still running the risk of not meeting demand, Mr. Behr said. That could trigger a cycle in which employees keep leaving and companies continue trying to fill vacancies with ever-greater urgency.\par \par \uc2 "The labor market is incredibly challenging in this hourly work world," said Mr. Behr.\par \par \uc2 The share of workers quitting transportation and warehousing jobs hit a high of 3.6% in August. It receded to 2.8% by October but was still above its February 2020 prepandemic rate of 2.4%.\par \par \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  is speeding up its hiring process for workers applying to certain positions in its stores and distribution centers. A candidate whose abilities align with the role can receive a contingent job offer, without an interview, as soon as 24 hours after applying, said Beth Marlowe, a \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  spokeswoman.\par \par \uc2 After receiving the contingent offer, the candidate is typically required to visit the store location, meet leadership and complete necessary paperwork.\par \par \uc2 "We've been piloting ways to accelerate our process for the last couple of years," Ms. Marlowe said.\par \par \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2  has always extended contingent job offers without interviews for hourly workers, according to a spokeswoman. Gap doesn't require interviews for some of its "no experience required" roles, including warehouse positions, a company spokeswoman said.\par \par \uc2 One recent posting for a \uc2 Macy's\uc2  seasonal retail sales associate in Holyoke, Mass., said new hires could "start right away!"\par \par \uc2 The role "may not require an in-person interview," the posting said.\par \par \uc2 Applications for seasonal roles at \uc2 Macy's\uc2  take as little as five minutes and a majority of job offers are made within 48 hours, said John Patterson, \uc2 Macy's\uc2  vice president of talent. "This speed is imperative during holiday hiring."\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich499\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal7485
