\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Strike at California Colleges Jeopardizes Exams\b0\par\par\uc2 By Douglas Belkin and Alicia A. Caldwell \par \uc2 696 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 December 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 LOS ANGELES -- Nearly 50,000 academic workers picketed 10 University of California campuses for a third week, generating uncertainty around final exams that are scheduled to begin next week, even as a quarter of the strikers reached a tentative deal this week.\par \par \uc2 The strike, one of the largest at an academic institution, has resulted in canceled midterms and classes across the 10 campuses. Strikers argue that the pandemic and inflation that followed exacerbated an already exploitative university business model that has long taken advantage of graduate students and contingent faculty who do the bulk of the teaching.\par \par \uc2 "We've been poorly paid for a long time, but the pandemic seemed to shift things, and we're finding it increasingly more difficult," said Rafael Jaime, who is pursuing a Ph.D. in medieval literature and serves as president of the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  bargaining unit that represents 19,000 graduate students and teaching assistants. "Wages have not kept up with inflation and so you're seeing more and more people pushed out of academia before they even finish a Ph.D."\par \par \uc2 The University of California said its "offers are generous, responsive to union priorities, and recognize the many valuable contributions of these employees."\par \par \uc2 Whether students will take final exams may depend on individual professors. Faculty have wide discretion with regard to grading, and on some campuses registrars have extended grade submission deadlines. The UCLA Academic Senate suggests professors could shorten final exams, make them multiple choice or offer "no harm" exams that would count only if they improve a student's grade.\par \par \uc2 The UC Academic Senate reminded faculty of their latitude and suggested contingencies including extending deadlines for final assignments, hiring temporary readers to help with grading and making final exams optional.\par \par \uc2 More than 200 professors have signed a petition pledging not to cross picket lines and have canceled classes to support the strike.\par \par \uc2 Most discussion sessions held by graduate student teachers have been canceled along with many midterm exams across the UC system, said Sky Yang, the student body president at \uc2 UC San Diego\uc2 . Mr. Yang was one of 10 student body presidents in the UC system to publicly support the strike but said that for the nearly 300,000 students in the UC system the strike has been more disruptive than the height of the pandemic -- when classes were held remotely.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Yang said he supports higher wages for graduate students but doesn't want higher tuition bills. "We already pay enough," he said.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the bargaining unit representing about a quarter of the strikers -- 12,000 postdoctoral students and researchers -- reached an agreement with UC. Postdocs will see a salary increase of up to 23% by next fall and $2,500 annual child-care subsidies. Union leaders said academic workers will remain on the picket lines until the contract is ratified.\par \par \uc2 Letitia Silas, the University of California's executive director of systemwide labor relations, praised the deal. "Our dedicated colleagues are vital to UC's research activities and we are very pleased to have reached agreements that honor their many important contributions," she said.\par \par \uc2 Graduate students make up nearly half of the striking workers and are demanding base salaries of $54,000; many earn less than half of that. Teaching assistants work no more than 20 hours a week and simultaneously pursue their degrees. The university proposed a new salary minimum of $24,874 for teaching fellows. Mr. Jaime said negotiations with his unit have been stalled for 12 days.\par \par \uc2 In addition to salary increases, workers are seeking child-care subsidies, better healthcare benefits and public-transportation benefits.\par \par \uc2 Gaurav Singh, a 21-year-old junior at \uc2 UC Santa Barbara\uc2 , said the strike has forced the university to reschedule his exams and now two are conflicting, forcing him to drop a class. The dropped class will mean Mr. Singh, an international student originally from India, will have to reconfigure his schedule for next quarter.\par \par \uc2 "I want to be able to graduate on time," he said. "There are still ways of working around this, it just puts me behind."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020221202eic200007\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
