\par\par \uc2 Heard on the Street\par \b \uc2 As Inflation Eases, Jobs Become More of A Fed Worry --- Bank has the cover to ratchet down rate rises\b0\par\par\uc2 By Justin Lahart \par \uc2 534 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 December 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 [Financial Analysis and Commentary]\par \par \uc2 Inflation is finally cooling. So is the job market. What matters now is which one cools fastest.\par \par \uc2 The Commerce Department on Thursday reported that its measure of consumer prices, which is the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's preferred inflation measure, rose 0.3% in October from a month earlier. That put it 6% above its year-earlier level, down from a June year-over-year peak of 7%, and the slimmest gain since last December.\par \par \uc2 More important, prices excluding food and energy items -- the so-called core that economists and policy makers focus on in an attempt to ascertain inflation's underlying trend -- climbed 0.2%.\par \par \uc2 Coming the day after Fed Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  signaled that the central bank will likely raise rates by half a percentage point at its next meeting, stepping down from its series of four 0.75-point rate increases, Thursday's report reinforced hopes that the Fed will be ending its tightening campaign sooner rather than later.\par \par \uc2 It seems likely that inflation will keep running cooler in the months ahead. Supply chains are running more smoothly, global commodity shortages have eased and many retailers are leaning on discounts to get rid of unwanted inventories. Moreover, private data show that rents are dropping, which should over time put a drag on housing-cost measures.\par \par \uc2 The growing concern now is the labor market. At the moment it appears quite healthy, with plenty of openings, new claims for unemployment insurance low, and hiring strong.\par \par \uc2 But the flurry of layoff announcements, though so far largely isolated to technology companies, is a concern.\par \par \uc2 And job growth has slowed: Economists forecast Friday's employment report will show the economy added 200,000 jobs last month which, though still a good number, would be the smallest gain in nearly two years.\par \par \uc2 Moreover, considering how much the Fed has already raised rates, and how rate increases take time to work their way through the economy, it is hard to imagine the labor market won't weaken more in the months ahead -- particularly if the Fed keeps ratcheting rates higher.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, Fed officials seem convinced that the job market needs to weaken to get inflation under control, with Mr. Powell on Wednesday saying the labor market "shows only tentative signs of rebalancing."\par \par \uc2 But milder inflation in the months ahead would allow the Fed to further reduce the magnitude of its rate increases, or even pause them, while it waits to see the full effect of its past rate increases. Moreover, it isn't entirely clear how much of the recent jump in inflation has to do with the job market and how much has to do with all the oddities brought on by the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 As part of its dual mandate, the Fed has been tasked by Congress with keeping inflation under control. But the other half of its mandate is to try to keep employment as high as possible without overheating the economy.\par \par \uc2 Inflation has been the central bank's main concern this year. Its focus might be about to shift.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich375\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal5625
