\par\par \b \uc2 Deal to Avert Railroad Strike Passes Senate, Heads to Biden\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katy Stech Ferek and Esther Fung \par \uc2 972 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 December 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- The Senate voted to intervene to prevent a nationwide strike by railroad workers while rejecting a proposal to give them expanded paid sick leave, with lawmakers saying they reluctantly heeded President Biden's call to resolve the long-running labor dispute.\par \par \uc2 In an 80-15 vote, with one senator voting present, lawmakers agreed to force unions to adopt an earlier labor agreement mediated by the administration. The measure now goes to the White House, and Mr. Biden said he would sign it as soon as it got to his desk.\par \par \uc2 The move is expected to end the standoff between \uc2 Union Pacific Corp\uc2 ., \uc2 CSX Corp\uc2 . and other freight railroads and more than 115,000 workers. Under the Railway Labor Act, Congress can make both sides accept an agreement to prevent harm to the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden as well as Republican and Democratic lawmakers said they didn't like getting in the middle of the dispute but that they couldn't risk a strike.\par \par \uc2 "Many in Congress shared my reluctance to override the union ratification procedures," Mr. Biden, a Democrat, said after the Senate vote. "But in this case, the consequences of a shutdown were just too great."\par \par \uc2 At the start of the week, Mr. Biden had pressed for urgent action by Congress and warned that a labor agreement needed to be set well before Dec. 9, when a cooling-off period expires and a strike could have begun.\par \par \uc2 Freight railroads and unions representing engineers, conductors, machinists and other workers have been in labor negotiations for more than two years. The White House appointed a mediation panel over the summer. Eight unions ratified a proposed contract that came out of those talks, while four didn't.\par \par \uc2 The main sticking points involved work schedules and paid sick time.\par \par \uc2 The five-year agreement, which replaces a contract that covers the period from 2015 to 2019, offers railroad workers a 24% increase in wages from 2020 through 2024. It allows for one additional paid day off, on top of existing vacation and paid time off.\par \par \uc2 Workers have been working under the terms of the old contract and will get back pay.\par \par \uc2 Business leaders -- spanning energy companies to fertilizer producers -- had sounded alarm bells over the possibility of a railroad strike, warning that a work stoppage or a lockout at the railroads could put hundreds of thousands out of work and exacerbate supply-chain problems and inflationary pressures.\par \par \uc2 "Averting a strike is a win for our country," said \uc2 Suzanne P. Clark\uc2 , president of the \uc2 U.S. Chamber of Commerce\uc2 . \uc2 Steve Lamar\uc2 , president and chief executive of the \uc2 American Apparel & Footwear Association\uc2 , said it is "critical for the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  to help America's supply chains restabilize."\par \par \uc2 Unions had criticized Mr. Biden's call for Congress to intervene, saying it undercut their bargaining position and would force some workers to accept a labor deal some had rejected.\par \par \uc2 Following the votes Thursday, the unions said they were disappointed by the rejection of paid sick leave and accused the senators who voted against that provision of cozying up to corporate donors.\par \par \uc2 The failure of the sick-leave vote "leaves me baffled, exasperated, and deeply saddened," said Tony Cardwell, national division president of the Brotherhood of Maintenance of Way Employes Division of the \uc2 International Brotherhood of Teamsters\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Almost every elected member of Congress campaigns on being 'for the working class'," said the Brotherhood of Railroad Signalmen. "The actions of many today demonstrated they are for the corporate class," the union said.\par \par \uc2 Once the labor agreement imposed by Congress is in place, rail workers aren't allowed to strike, and they have been warned by their unions that such a move is tantamount to signaling their resignation.\par \par \uc2 The trade group representing railroads said the move by Washington would provide certainty, casting the imposed agreement as a compromise that provides substantial gains for workers.\par \par \uc2 "All rail stakeholders and the economy writ large now have certainty about the path forward," said Ian Jefferies, president and CEO of the \uc2 Association of American Railroads\uc2 , adding "there is more to be done to further address our employees' work-life balance concerns."\par \par \uc2 A strike would have cost the U.S. economy about $2 billion a day, according to the association.\par \par \uc2 Intervention by Congress is rare in recent decades. The last national rail strike, in 1991, lasted about 24 hours before Congress passed, and then-President George H.W. Bush signed, legislation ordering workers back to their jobs.\par \par \uc2 The Senate action came after House lawmakers had passed two measures on Wednesday, one that would force the unions to implement the labor deal and one that would give union workers seven additional paid sick days. The Senate then considered both on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The vote for additional paid sick days was defeated, despite support from almost all Democrats and a half-dozen Republicans, in a tally that scrambled party and ideological lines. The vote was 52 in favor to 43 against, short of the 60 needed to pass.\par \par \uc2 The six Republicans who voted in favor of expanded benefits were Sens. Ted Cruz of Texas, \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  of Missouri, \uc2 Mike Braun\uc2  of Indiana, \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  of South Carolina, \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  of Florida and John Kennedy of Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 Other Republicans said they didn't support the additional leave. Sens. \uc2 Kevin Cramer\uc2  of North Dakota and \uc2 Cynthia Lummis\uc2  of Wyoming said that while they support forced adoption of the agreement, they "cannot support an agreement that goes beyond what was negotiated by all parties."\par \par \uc2 The 15 senators who voted against imposing the labor deal ranged from progressives such as Elizabeth Warren (D., Mass.) and Bernie Sanders (I., Vt.) to conservatives including Messrs. Cruz and Hawley.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Lindsay Wise and Catherine Lucey contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020221202eic20006u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
