\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: HarperCollins Union Strikes Over Wages and Benefits\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jeffrey A. Trachtenberg \par \uc2 432 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 November 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A union representing approximately 250 members at \uc2 HarperCollins Publishers\uc2  went on an open-ended strike Thursday morning in an effort to secure higher wages and improved benefits.\par \par \uc2 The union, Local 2110 of the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 , put up a picket line outside the company's headquarters in lower Manhattan. Employees represented by the union include editorial, sales and publicity staffers. \uc2 HarperCollins Publishers\uc2  employs about 4,000 people globally.\par \par \uc2 "There is never a good time for a strike, but we have to do it," said Olga Brudastova, president of the local. "We've been in negotiations since mid-December and working without a contract since April."\par \par \uc2 Ms. Brudastova said the local received an email Nov. 3 from Zandra Magarino, senior vice president, people, at \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2 , in which Ms. Magarino said the local shouldn't expect the company's latest economic proposal to change. That position led the local to go on strike, Ms. Brudastova said.\par \par \uc2 A spokeswoman for \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  said the publisher "has agreed to a number of proposals that the \uc2 United Auto Workers Union\uc2  is seeking to include in a new contract." \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2 , like The Wall Street Journal, is owned by \uc2 News Corp\uc2 . "We are disappointed an agreement has not been reached and will continue to negotiate in good faith," the spokeswoman said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Magarino on Nov. 4 sent a memo to \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  employees in which she said entry-level salaries rose by 25% since the last signed contract. She said \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  recently gave merit raises to staffers, including members of the \uc2 UAW\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 News Corp\uc2  on Nov. 8 reported segment earnings before interest, taxes, depreciation and amortization at \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  fell 54% to $39 million on an 11% decline in revenue for the quarter ended Sept. 30. \uc2 News Corp\uc2  attributed the poor results primarily to a decision by \uc2 Amazon.com Inc\uc2 . to reduce inventory levels and close warehouses. The cutbacks led to lower sales of physical books.\par \par \uc2 The union held a one-day strike in July over pay and benefits issues, contending that union members earned $55,000 on average, with the lowest-paid employees earning $45,000. "We didn't make progress after our one-day strike," said Laura Harshberger, a senior production editor. "This is what everybody wanted to do. It isn't about one person's pay but about making \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  a better place to work."\par \par \uc2 The new strike started at about 9 a.m. Thursday. An estimated 50 to 75 pickets at 195 Broadway carried signs that read "Union Strong" and "Union Workers Want Fair Wages."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020221111eibb0000l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
