\par\par \b \uc2 The New Workplace (A Special Report) --- When a Boss Wants New Ideas, Employees Respond. If, That Is, the Boss Is a Man: A study finds that employees are surprised when men profess to not know the answer\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lisa Ward \par \uc2 637 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 November 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par R8\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 When a boss is curious about something, employees are likely to feel secure, voice their opinions and take risks -- if, that is, the boss is a man.\par \par \uc2 But when a female boss displays curiosity, it often goes unnoticed, according to a recent study.\par \par \uc2 A curious boss, as defined by the study's two authors, is someone who is eager to learn, understand and challenge existing theories, develop new strategies, improve work processes and look for new solutions. The authors found that male bosses exhibiting these traits have a much bigger impact on employee behavior than female bosses do.\par \par \uc2 In the study, employees with curious male bosses were three to four times as likely as employees with curious female bosses to report that they felt comfortable making suggestions or giving opinions. Similarly, the former were two to three times as likely to speak their minds -- recommending new projects or procedures -- as their counterparts with curious female bosses.\par \par \uc2 "It's like a dancing bear," says study co-author Phillip S. Thompson, an assistant professor at \uc2 Virginia Tech\uc2 's Pamplin College of Business. Just as the bear isn't expected to dance, Dr. Thompson says, men traditionally aren't expected to admit they might not know the answer to a question. Thus, when men are curious, their employees are likely to notice and react.\par \par \uc2 Women, on the other hand, traditionally are expected to be empathic, so their curiosity tends to go more unnoticed.\par \par \uc2 The result: Male leaders get a lot of extra credit for being curious even when their female colleagues exhibit the same exact behavior.\par \par \uc2 The authors surveyed 149 managers and 1,123 of their direct reports across four companies: two in healthcare, an education-consulting firm and one in financial services.\par \par \uc2 The first survey asked workers to rate their managers' curiosity. Seven weeks later, the workers were asked about how psychologically safe they felt at work and if they were willing to take risks. The final survey, given 14 weeks after the first, asked managers to evaluate whether their subordinates often spoke up at work, even if it meant going against colleagues' opinions.\par \par \uc2 The authors found some benefit for curious female leaders at some companies. Specifically, at the education-consulting and financial-services companies, workers with curious female leaders reported a statistically significant level of psychological safety. But even then, employees with male leaders at these same companies felt about five times as safe. Employees in the healthcare organizations reported no statistically significant psychological benefit from having a curious female leader.\par \par \uc2 The authors also found no downside to being a curious leader, regardless of the leaders' gender. In other words, curiosity was never correlated with employees feeling unsafe or being afraid to speak up.\par \par \uc2 "Curiosity really is a leadership superpower," says Anthony Klotz, the paper's other co-author and an associate professor at UCL School of Management at \uc2 University College London\uc2 . "When leaders are curious, they signal that it is safe for employees to learn and explore."\par \par \uc2 Women, Dr. Klotz adds, should see the same benefits from their curiosity as their male colleagues. To make that happen, the authors recommend that companies teach employees about unconscious bias toward female leaders, so they can be more aware of the double standard female leaders often face.\par \par \uc2 "Women leaders should not have to face additional headwinds," Dr. Klotz says, "especially since companies increasingly want employees to speak up when they have creative solutions, see a problem or are struggling with other issues, like mental-health challenges."\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Ms. Ward is a writer in Vermont. Email her at reports@wsj.com.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020221109eib900007\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
