\par\par \b \uc2 Prison-Labor Bans Are About Unions More Than 'Slavery'\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chandra Bozelko \par \uc2 619 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 November 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 I worked in York Correctional Institution's kitchen for five years. I earned 75 cents a day for the first year, then got a raise to $1.75 a day. That job is the reason I'm alive. The work was menial, but it provided physical and mental escape from the Connecticut prison cell where I was serving a sentence for larceny, identity theft and improper use of a credit card.\par \par \uc2 A movement against prison labor seems to be gaining ground. The 13th Amendment outlawed slavery and involuntary servitude, "except as a punishment for crime whereof the party shall have been duly convicted." The constitutions of 19 states have similar language. On Tuesday voters in five of them -- Alabama, Louisiana, Oregon, Tennessee and Vermont -- will decide whether to rewrite their charters to abolish that exception.\par \par \uc2 Colorado voters led the way by passing a similar ballot measure in 2018; Nebraska and Utah followed in 2020. So far these measures have had no effect on the state's prison-labor programs, although litigation is pending against Colorado's. If it succeeds, the state may have to pay prisoners minimum wage if they work at all.\par \par \uc2 But voters should pay attention to who would benefit from this alleged reform to prison labor. It isn't the inmates. According to a report from \uc2 the American Civil Liberties Union\uc2 , two-thirds of state and federal inmates work and would descend into idleness if courts hold that the new state constitutions require them to be paid. The California Legislature refused to strike the language from its constitution earlier this year because lawmakers feared that doing so would eventually require the state to pay 65,000 inmates the minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 Some 80% of prison labor isn't performed under contract to private companies; it's maintenance work for the facilities themselves. If prisons have to pay minimum wage, they will hire more workers who aren't incarcerated -- employees who can be organized into unions. The \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  has held that inmates can form unions but wardens have far more discretion to bust them than do employers on the outside.\par \par \uc2 Prison labor has been controversial since President \uc2 Franklin D. Roosevelt\uc2  formalized it in 1934, but not always because of the 13th Amendment. Unions took issue with what they viewed as unfair low-wage competition for their members and prospective members. The National Center for Policy Analysis reports that organized labor has closed down prison industries in Arizona, Illinois, Ohio and Texas.\par \par \uc2 Historically, sympathy for incarcerated workers has heightened when the community around a prison started losing money. In 1879 in Galveston, Texas, townspeople showed little concern for prisoners's well-being until the prison stopped purchasing goods from local suppliers; then concerns surfaced that inmates were being abused. In 1891 union miners freed the incarcerated laborers working for the Knoxville Iron Co. -- only to force the mine to rehire union members.\par \par \uc2 It may seem unfair to give jobs to prisoners when law-abiding citizens are out of work, but this argument is less compelling at a time like now, when employers are desperate to hire.\par \par \uc2 The constitutional language that seems to authorize slavery as punishment is embarrassing, but prisoners aren't really enslaved -- they're not treated as legal property of the state or anyone else. And work experience is a crucial element to rehabilitation. Utah and Colorado both employ incarcerated inmates in work assignments outside prison to ease tight labor markets. My humble job in the prison kitchen helped prepare me for life on the outside.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Ms. Bozelko is a columnist for the National Memo and author of the blog Prison Diaries.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020221105eib50001c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
