\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Home Buyers Set Sights Farther Away --- Median distance people move rises to 50 miles from 15 in era of high prices and remote work\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicole Friedman \par \uc2 822 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 November 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The rise of remote work and the ballooning cost of housing in major metro areas are leading Americans to move much farther away when buying a home.\par \par \uc2 Buyers who purchased homes in the year ended in June moved a median of 50 miles from their previous residences, according to a National Association of Realtors survey released Thursday. That distance is the highest on record in annual data going back to 2005 and follows five straight years in which the median distance moved was constant at 15 miles, \uc2 NAR\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 The moving distance likely soared because many employers solidified their in-office requirements, giving some remote workers the certainty to move farther from their offices, said Jessica Lautz, \uc2 NAR\uc2 's vice president of research. In mid-2021, "people had the freedom and the ability with the low interest rates to say, 'I'm going to make this move now,' " Ms. Lautz said.\par \par \uc2 Stanton and Donna Zeff had planned to move to Florida when Mr. Zeff retired in about six years. Instead, they decided to make the move sooner because Mr. Zeff can now work remotely as a program manager. "I can do my job out of the office just as well as I can do it in the office," he said.\par \par \uc2 They sold their home near Dallas in January and recently bought a four-bedroom house in Davenport, Fla., which includes a suite for Mr. Zeff's mother, who will be moving with them.\par \par \uc2 Smaller communities were especially popular with buyers: 48% of home purchases were in small towns and rural areas, a record in data going back to 2003 and up from 32% a year earlier, the \uc2 NAR\uc2  survey showed.\par \par \uc2 Suburbs are traditionally the most popular destinations for home buyers, but the share of suburban home purchases dropped to 39% from 51% the prior year, according to \uc2 NAR\uc2 . Only 10% of purchases were urban areas, down from 13% the year before.\par \par \uc2 NAR\uc2  polled about 4,850 people who bought primary homes during the 12-month period. \uc2 News Corp\uc2 , owner of The Wall Street Journal, also operates Realtor.com under license from \uc2 NAR\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The survey reflects a 12-month period in which the housing market was upended by the surge in interest rates. The Covid-19 pandemic spurred the biggest housing boom in years as people took advantage of record-low mortgage rates and sought more space to work from home. Home prices soared to record highs. Now that mortgage rates have more than doubled, home purchases have dwindled and buyers have focused more on affordability.\par \par \uc2 The typical distance moved rose more for repeat buyers than for first-time buyers in the year ended in June, Ms. Lautz said. Most repeat buyers have benefited in recent years from rising home prices, enabling them to sell their homes at a profit and put the proceeds into their next purchase.\par \par \uc2 Dana and Madison Christoffersen, who lived with their two children in Livermore, Calif., sold their home in April for $1.25 million. Six months later, they bought a bigger, newly built house in Nampa, Idaho, for $585,000, Mr. Christoffersen said.\par \par \uc2 He got a new job in Idaho in the construction industry, and his parents, who lived in California, also moved to Idaho. The family wanted a slower pace of life and lower cost of living, Mr. Christoffersen said.\par \par \uc2 "We're really excited about it," Mr. Christoffersen said. "We could afford a big, nice house in Idaho."\par \par \uc2 For other buyers, the opportunity for remote work meant they could exit more crowded and expensive places such as the Bay Area, Los Angeles and New York City. Those metro areas ranked among the biggest net loss of home buyers between March 2020 and February 2022, according to an analysis by mortgage-finance company \uc2 Freddie Mac\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Frances and Erik Larios left San Jose, Calif., for the Boise, Idaho, area with their daughter in January 2020 because of Mrs. Larios's job relocation. But during the pandemic, they missed their families in California. The couple, who are now both able to work remotely, sold their Idaho house in June and bought a home in Fresno, Calif., in July.\par \par \uc2 The metro area with the highest net migration of home buyers between March 2020 and February 2022 was Riverside, Calif., about 50 miles from Los Angeles, according to \uc2 Freddie Mac\uc2 . North Port, Fla., about 80 miles from Tampa, also attracted many home buyers.\par \par \uc2 The trend toward cheaper housing markets looks poised to continue, said \uc2 Ben Ayers\uc2 , senior economist at \uc2 Nationwide Insurance\uc2 . Even if home prices decline from current levels, "I don't think that, unfortunately, we're going to get back to the levels of affordability that we've gotten used to over the past five or six years," he said.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich467\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal7005
