\par\par \b \uc2 Service to Ease 401(k) Rollovers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anne Tergesen \par \uc2 624 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 October 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Millions of workers have changed jobs in the past few years. A large share of them have likely left retirement savings behind or cashed out their accounts.\par \par \uc2 Cash-outs result in billions of dollars lost to future retirement savings, as workers opt to spend the money, paying taxes and penalties on it rather than rolling it to a new employer's 401(k) plan or keeping it invested in the old plan.\par \par \uc2 To reduce those cash-outs and boost retirement savings, three of the nation's largest 401(k) plan administrators are jointly offering a way for employers to enable the automatic transfer of small retirement accounts of departing workers to a new employer's retirement-savings plan.\par \par \uc2 Fidelity Investments\uc2 , \uc2 Vanguard Group\uc2  and \uc2 Alight Solutions LLC\uc2  -- which collectively represent about 40% of the 401(k) industry's participants -- announced the joint effort Wednesday. They say the service will especially benefit people in minority groups, women and low-income workers who disproportionately cash out accounts after leaving jobs.\par \par \uc2 The automatic rollovers have been available since 2021, albeit on a relatively small scale. The consortium, called Portability Services Network LLC, plans to make automatic transfers available, starting in the first quarter of 2023, to more than 48,000 401(k)-type plans that Fidelity, Alight and \uc2 Vanguard\uc2  administer. In total, nearly 44 million workers are in those plans.\par \par \uc2 Fidelity\uc2  and other 401(k) providers offering the service won't require employers to offer the feature, but "we're optimistic about adoption," said Kevin Barry, head of the workplace-benefits business at \uc2 Fidelity\uc2 , the nation's largest plan administrator.\par \par \uc2 Robert Johnson\uc2 , majority owner of Retirement Clearinghouse LLC, a private company that pioneered the rollover technology and is joining the consortium, said that because high percentages of minority workers cash out retirement savings when changing jobs, automatic transfers could help close the racial gap when it comes to retirement savings.\par \par \uc2 Automatically transferring small 401(k) accounts would reduce cash-outs and potentially save participants an additional $1.5 trillion over 40 years, according to the nonprofit \uc2 Employee Benefit Research Institute\uc2 . That would include $619 billion for Black and minority workers.\par \par \uc2 Retirement Clearinghouse will have a 51% stake in the new entity.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Johnson, the founder of Black Entertainment Television LLC, will serve as chairman. \uc2 Fidelity\uc2 , \uc2 Vanguard\uc2  and Alight will evenly divide the remaining 49% stake, said Spencer Williams, chief executive of Retirement Clearinghouse.\par \par \uc2 "We felt this was the best structure to address what is a massive industry problem" of lost savings, \uc2 Fidelity\uc2 's Mr. Barry said.\par \par \uc2 The consortium will charge a price that covers its costs, Mr. Barry said, and use any profits it generates to reduce the fees employees pay on transfers.\par \par \uc2 The consortium plans initially to charge workers a maximum one-time fee of $30 for each transfer. For accounts with $600 or less, it will charge 5% of the account value. The service is free to accounts with $50 or less, said Mr. Williams. Previously, Retirement Clearinghouse charged up to $59 per transfer.\par \par \uc2 Fidelity\uc2 , Alight and \uc2 Vanguard\uc2  stand to benefit from keeping more of workers' money in 401(k) accounts, since they charge fees on the assets in retirement plans.\par \par \uc2 The rollover service automatically transfers accounts of departing employees with balances of $5,000 or less. For higher balances, an employee's permission is generally required before money is transferred.\par \par \uc2 When the service finds a 401(k) account from an employee's former workplace, it notifies the worker of a match. Unless the employee opts out of the transfer, the service moves the money to the person's current 401(k) account.\par \par \uc2 "It makes it easy to say no to a cash-out," Mr. Johnson said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020221006eia600012\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
