\par\par \b \uc2 Labor Unrest Weighs on Supply Chains\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Berger and Liz Young \par \uc2 460 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 September 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 From seaport docks in Los Angeles and Liverpool to rail yards in Chicago and warehouses in Europe and the U.S., clashes between cargo workers and management have been rising this year, adding complications and uncertainty to the flow of goods around the world.\par \par \uc2 A two-week strike launched Tuesday by dockworkers at Liverpool port in England is the latest in a series of walkouts to hit the U.K.'s transportation networks. It follows last week's agreement in the U.S. that averted a potential nationwide freight rail strike hours before it was set to begin.\par \par \uc2 The conflicts count a range of workers that handle freight around the world, from truck drivers in South Korea to package sorters at \uc2 Amazon.com Inc\uc2 . distribution centers in New York, who are newly emboldened to press for higher pay and better working conditions after more than two years of stressed business during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Frontline\uc2  workers are "tired of being called essential and treated as expendable," said \uc2 Liz Shuler\uc2 , president of the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 , the largest federation of labor unions in the U.S. "I do think this is emblematic of what we have seen and what we are going to continue to see."\par \par \uc2 Some of the labor battles, including the strikes at Liverpool and at the \uc2 Port of Felixstowe\uc2  in England, have shut down freight transport operations for a time. Others, such as the near-strike against U.S. railroads and continuing contract talks involving U.S. West Coast dockworkers, have triggered contingency planning by businesses bracing for broader impact.\par \par \uc2 High inflation is adding to pressure for higher wages. Workers at Liverpool's port rejected a contract offer that included an 8.3% annual pay increase, and the \uc2 Unite union\uc2  said that fell short of increases in consumer prices.\par \par \uc2 In South Korea, truck drivers snarled the country's export-driven supply chains in June when they stopped working for a week in a dispute over pay and the drivers' demand for subsidies to cover rising fuel costs.\par \par \uc2 Companies say the conflicts have made logistics planning more difficult and exacerbated strains caused by supply-chain shocks such as the recent Covid-19 shutdowns in China and the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 "It doesn't take a lot of disruption to cause massive effects on supply chains and incur additional costs for importers and exporters worldwide," said Morten Landry, managing director for air and sea in the U.K. and Ireland for Danish freight forwarder DSV AS.\par \par \uc2 DSV in recent months has navigated strikes at Felixstowe, Britain's busiest container port, and Hamburg, Germany. The company tries to rapidly shift cargo to alternative ports when a gateway closes, Mr. Landry said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220922ei9m0001l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
