\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Opponents Work to Block Fast-Food Law\b0\par\par\uc2 By Christine Mai-Duc and Heather Haddon \par \uc2 729 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 September 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Restaurants owners, business groups and other opponents of a new California law that could set the minimum wage for the fast-food industry as high as $22 an hour next year have begun an effort to postpone its implementation and let voters decide whether to permanently block it in 2024.\par \par \uc2 A proposed referendum seeking to overturn the law, known as the FAST Recovery Act, was filed with the California attorney general's office Tuesday afternoon, a spokeswoman for the state's top lawyer confirmed.\par \par \uc2 A coalition, called Protect Neighborhood Restaurants, said Wednesday that it was backing the effort to let voters weigh in on the law. The \uc2 International Franchise Association\uc2  and the \uc2 National Restaurant Association\uc2 , two Washington, D.C.-based industry groups, were co-chairing the coalition, the group said.\par \par \uc2 "It is only right for California's voters to have a voice before harboring the burden of a bill that has widely been heralded as a massive step in the wrong direction," the coalition said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 The FAST Recovery Act was signed into law by Gov. \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , a Democrat, on Monday. It is set to take effect Jan. 1, but would be suspended if the referendum, which would likely be on the ballot in November 2024, qualifies.\par \par \uc2 The law, the first of its kind in the nation, calls for California to create a 10-person council including workers, union representatives, employers and business advocates that could set a minimum wage for fast-food workers next year as high as $22. That minimum would rise annually based on inflation.\par \par \uc2 California's current minimum wage is $15 an hour and is set to increase by 50 cents next year.\par \par \uc2 Organized labor backed the new law as a way to improve wages in an industry that unions have struggled to organize in part due to the large number of franchisees. They said they were now prepared to defend it.\par \par \uc2 "We are not counting on it qualifying," \uc2 Mary Kay Henry\uc2 , international president of the \uc2 Service Employees International Union\uc2 , said of the proposed referendum at a news conference Wednesday, "but we're also prepared for a fight."\par \par \uc2 Fast-food corporations and their franchisees accused legislators of singling them out and said forcing them to raise wages so quickly would increase their operating costs abruptly and cause prices to rise industrywide.\par \par \uc2 "Unfortunately, in an already high-inflation environment, we'll now be forced to pass along most of the wage increase to customers, at least in the short term," said Anand Gowda, founder of San Diego-based High Bluff Capital Partners, which owns the \uc2 Church's Chicken\uc2  and Quiznos chains. Mr. Gowda said he is looking to automate operations in his restaurants to reduce staff because of the law.\par \par \uc2 Restaurant and business groups spent at least $1.7 million to lobby against the bill after it was introduced last year and last week urged Mr. Newsom to veto it.\par \par \uc2 Amendments added at the end of the legislative session last month and negotiated with input from Mr. Newsom's administration curtailed some of the council's authority and added the $22 wage cap, as well as a 2028 sunset that would shut down the council unless renewed.\par \par \uc2 In a video posted to social media after he signed the bill into law on Labor Day, Mr. Newsom said he was proud to sign a measure that "empowers our workers."\par \par \uc2 Opponents will need to submit roughly 623,000 valid voter signatures by Dec. 4 to qualify a referendum for the November 2024 ballot. Typically in California, referendum backers spend millions to gather the signatures needed to get onto the ballot and need to collect more than the minimum, as some end up being disqualified.\par \par \uc2 The spokeswoman for the attorney general said her office will issue a title and summary for the measure, necessary for proponents to begin collecting signatures, by Sept. 16.\par \par \uc2 If it qualifies, the referendum could be a costly fight between organized labor and the fast-food industry resembling 2020's Proposition 22, in which companies including \uc2 Uber Technologies Inc\uc2 ., \uc2 Lyft Inc\uc2 . and \uc2 DoorDash Inc\uc2 . convinced voters to exempt them from a law meant to classify gig workers as full-time employees. The companies and labor spent some $220 million advertising for and against the measure.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220908ei980000t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
